\begin{didyouknow}{Periodograms for Messy Data}
Astronomers rarely get evenly spaced data, because clouds, daylight and seasons interrupt observing. Nicholas Lomb in 1976 and Jeffrey Scargle in 1982 developed a periodogram that fits sinusoids directly to irregularly timed points. The Lomb-Scargle periodogram is now used to find periods of variable stars and exoplanet signals by scanning a dense grid of trial frequencies.
\end{didyouknow}
