\begin{equation}
\sin\bigl(\omega_j(t+P)\bigr)=\sin\bigl(\omega_jt+j\Delta\omega\cdot\tfrac{2\pi}{\Delta\omega}\bigr)=\sin(\omega_jt+2\pi j)=\sin(\omega_jt),
\end{equation}
and likewise for cosine. Every term repeats after $P$, so the whole model does:
\begin{equation}
F(t+P)=F(t),\qquad P=\frac{2\pi}{\Delta\omega}=2\pi\cdot\frac{450}{8}\approx353.4 .
\label{eq:sparse-frequency-period}
\end{equation}
\emph{Whatever} amplitudes are fitted, the C1 model is a periodic function with period about $353.4$ in $t$. The true fluctuation is not periodic: it is a sum of sinusoids at the mutually incommensurate frequencies $\log q$, an almost-periodic function that never repeats (Chapter~\ref{ch:fourier-series}).

