Shift $t$ by $T_{\rm rep}=2\pi/\Delta\omega$. (We write $T_{\rm rep}$, for ``repeat'', to keep $P$ for the prime set and $T$ for the window length.)
\begin{equation}
\sin\bigl(\omega_j(t+T_{\rm rep})\bigr)=\sin\bigl(\omega_jt+j\Delta\omega\cdot\tfrac{2\pi}{\Delta\omega}\bigr)=\sin(\omega_jt+2\pi j)=\sin(\omega_jt),
\end{equation}
and likewise for cosine. Every term repeats after $T_{\rm rep}$, so the whole model does too:
\begin{equation}
F(t+T_{\rm rep})=F(t),\qquad T_{\rm rep}=\frac{2\pi}{\Delta\omega}=2\pi\cdot\frac{450}{8}\approx353.4 .
\label{eq:sparse-frequency-period}
\end{equation}
\emph{Whatever} amplitudes are fitted, the C1 model is a periodic function with period about $353.4$ in $t$. The true fluctuation is not periodic. It is a sum of sinusoids whose base frequencies $\log p$, for distinct primes $p$, are mutually incommensurate, together with their harmonics $m\log p$. The base frequencies are incommensurate because $\log p/\log p'=a/b$ with integers $a,b$ would give $p^b=p'^a$, which is impossible for distinct primes by unique factorization (Chapter~\ref{ch:primes}). Such a sum is almost periodic and never repeats (Chapter~\ref{ch:fourier-series}).

