\emph{Prediction error.} Over a stretch of $t$ short enough that $\phi$ is nearly constant but long enough to contain many periods, $\theta$ sweeps uniformly through all angles. The difference between prediction and truth is
\begin{equation}
A\sin(\theta+\phi)-A\sin\theta=2A\cos\bigl(\theta+\tfrac\phi2\bigr)\sin\tfrac\phi2\quad\text{($\sin\alpha-\sin\beta=2\cos\tfrac{\alpha+\beta}2\sin\tfrac{\alpha-\beta}2$)}.
\end{equation}
The mean of $\cos^2$ over all angles is $\tfrac12$, so the mean square error is $4A^2\sin^2(\phi/2)\cdot\tfrac12$, and
\begin{equation}
\text{rms error}=\sqrt2\,A\,\abs{\sin(\phi/2)} .
\end{equation}
The null prediction (zero) has rms error equal to the rms of $A\sin\theta$, which is $A/\sqrt2$. The ratio is
\begin{equation}
\frac{\text{rms error}}{\text{null rms}}=2\abs{\sin(\phi/2)} .
\label{eq:sparse-frequency-rmsratio}
\end{equation}

\emph{Correlation.} The mean of $\sin\theta\sin(\theta+\phi)=\tfrac12[\cos\phi-\cos(2\theta+\phi)]$ over all angles is $\tfrac12\cos\phi$; each factor alone has mean square $\tfrac12$ and mean zero. Dividing the covariance by the product of the standard deviations (Chapter~\ref{ch:probability}),
\begin{equation}
\corr=\frac{\tfrac12A^2\cos\phi}{\sqrt{\tfrac12A^2}\sqrt{\tfrac12A^2}}=\cos\phi .
\label{eq:sparse-frequency-corr}
\end{equation}

\emph{Consequences.} At $\phi=0$ the prediction is perfect. As $\phi$ grows, the correlation falls as $\cos\phi$ and the error grows. The prediction is worse than the null once $2\abs{\sin(\phi/2)}>1$, that is, once $\phi>\pi/3$; at $\phi=\pi/2$ the correlation is zero; at $\phi=\pi$ the prediction is anticorrelated and twice as bad as the null.

